The handler may take at most one argument and may return a result. The generated caller marshals the call onto the service's own site (scheduler) and, when the handler returns a value, exposes it as an awaitable result.
[ServiceRequest] // request "TriggerSomething" protected void Handle_TriggerSomething() { }
[ServiceRequest] // request "UpdateAValue" protected void Handle_UpdateAValue(int val) { }

             Base for a clock-ungated service — one that owns external I/O (a hardware link, an
             out-of-process feed) that runs outside simulation time and acts on the run's start/stop, e.g. an
             EGSE that halts/unhalts a real OBC in lockstep with the sim. Its  is a
             : a dedicated dispatcher thread that serially runs the work
             marshalled to it (its generated [ServiceRequest]/[ServiceEvent] plumbing, plus
             anything the service Posts) and sleeps when idle. There is no author-written loop.


             The design is an actor with its own I/O threads:


Dispatcher (the site thread) — owns the service's single-threaded state and does
               outbound sends inline from a marshalled request handler (a different socket than
               the reader, so it never contends with a read). No locks: all state lives here.

Reader thread(s) — the service spins these itself (a blocking socket read) and hands
               inbound work back to the dispatcher via .Post(...). Bound to the run via a
               : (re)spawn in ,
               cancel + join in .

Control plane — / fire
               inline on the clock thread (not the dispatcher), so a halt/unhalt over an independent
               control link is immediate and never queued behind data work.

             The canonical shape:

             public override void Configure() => _link = Connect();        // open sockets; hardware halted

             protected override void OnRunStarted()                        // clock thread: unhalt + start reading
             {
                 _link.Unhalt();
                 _rx = new CancellationTokenSource();
                 _rxThread = new Thread(() => ReadLoop(_rx.Token)) { IsBackground = true };
                 _rxThread.Start();
             }
             protected override void OnRunStopped()                        // clock thread: halt + stop reading
             {
                 _link.Halt(); _rx?.Cancel(); _link.WakeReader(); _rxThread?.Join();
             }

             [ServiceRequest] protected void Handle_Ch0Tx(byte[] f) => _link.Write(0, f);  // dispatcher: inline send
             private void ReadLoop(CancellationToken ct)
             {
                 while (!ct.IsCancellationRequested && _link.TryRead(out var ch, out var frame))
                     Site.Post(() => Route(ch, frame));                    // hand inbound to the dispatcher
             }

             (The link's read and write paths must be independent — separate sockets — because the reader thread
             and the dispatcher touch them concurrently.)

Schedules a coroutine process, first stepped  µs from now;
the  is materialized at that first step and each yield
re-schedules the next step by its delay.
